house of the dragon gives us the iron  throne we deserve  game of thrones turned the iron throne  into a pop culture icon but house of the  dragon gives us a more grandiose and  menacing version of the coveted chair  fans of the game of thrones novels and  tv series have been waiting for the  debut trailer of the long-awaited  prequel house of dragons and it finally  came  this time we're going back to the time  where westeros was ruled by house  targaryen with fire and blood  one of the most outstanding things about  the debut trailer of house of the dragon  is that we get a glimpse of the iron  throne but it looks very different from  what we remember  this royal seat is the symbol of  supremacy and now it looks more the part  it's a more faithful reflection of  george rr martin's vision so it looks a  lot more intimidating  the iron throne construction  over a century before the events of  house of dragons balerion the black  dread which was aegon the conqueror's  dragon melted one thousand blades with  his fiery breath  which was then worked on by blacksmiths  to make the iron throne  the blades were surrendered to the  targaryens by their defeated enemies in  the war of conquest so the iron throne  became a symbol of their superiority  george r.r martin has always said that  the iron throne is a mammoth of a seat  he describes it as towering over the  room and it's not necessarily aesthetic  or comfortable to sit on  it's an asymmetrical ugly beast which  makes sense considering it was made by a  dragon and blacksmiths not expert  furniture manufacturers  it's worth noting that though the house  of the dragon gives us a more  threatening version of the iron throne  it's still not exactly as described by  martin's words  if we go back to the novels the royal  seat is over 10 feet tall  so steps have to be climbed to reach it  because it's so tall it gives its  occupant a looming almost godly presence  and the sun coming from the windows and  the red keep cast a sinister shadow  the iron throne is simply frightening  and very much impractical especially  because the melted swords are still  sharp  and they poke out  additionally the fang steel behind makes  it impossible for the occupant to sit  back  the royal seat is intentionally  uncomfortable because aegon first  claimed a king should never sit easy  an evil seat  the iron throne is meant to be a visual  representation of bad vibes and  according to martin mark simonetti's  take on the royal seat truly captures  what he intended to portray in the  novels  of course the artist simonetti still  made some changes to make it more  practical for the show  but overall he captured the evil  aesthetic martin was going for  martin's work has repeatedly focused on  how impractical the structure of the  iron throne is and the effects he can  have on people's psyche  a feast for crows explains this very  well  ares the second targaryen also known as  the mad king is a great example of that  because he was essentially tormented by  the throne  the mad king was covered in half-heeled  cuts and scabs from his time on the seat  so one could say that the throne  constantly tortured him and it's not  difficult to see how it could be a  contributing factor to his madness  that madness would become the reason for  robert's rebellion which would lead to  the eventual destruction of the house  targaryen  far from game of thrones iron throne  the house of the dragons take of the  iconic seat is very different from what  we've seen on game of thrones  the version we know is a minimal  interpretation of martin's vision it's a  seat made of swords but we can say it  failed to make the right impression  it was simply too ergonomic to portray  the true evil and torturous nature of  the seat additionally it lacked the  steep stairs which were substituted for  a layered dye that was made from stone  not iron so it didn't look like a part  of the throne itself  the show was aware of these differences  leading to a kind of meta exchange  between littlefinger and varus  where littlefinger says he's counted the  swords on the throne and there aren't  even 200 of them  though the exchange may have gone over  the heads of those who hadn't read the  novels by the time the tv series aired  it served as a canonical explanation for  the differences  of course the reason behind these  differences is much simpler they didn't  have the budget in the beginning to  bring martin's vision of the iron throne  to life more accurately  especially because they had to reshoot  the pilot which put a big dent on the  budget  additionally it was just cumbersome to  make the iron throne truer to the novels  they would have had to build a larger  set for it and actors would have had to  constantly navigate around a dangerous  10-foot set piece that posed more risks  than it should  conclusion  it is very exciting to see what the  house of the dragon will do with this  more authentic representation of the  royal seat  of course the iron throne will be the  least of the character's worries in this  prequel which will focus on the internal  issues of house targaryen the demise of  the dragons and the royal civil war that  would devastate westeros  starring matt smith as prince damon  targaryen emma darcy as princess  rhaenyra targaryen and patty considine  as king viserys the first among many  others  this tv series takes place 200 years  before the events of game of thrones  and we expect it to be nothing short of  epic  though we don't know exactly when house  of the dragon will premiere just yet  because they haven't revealed the date  we can expect the first season in 2022  on hbo and hbo max hopefully it will do  the house of fire and blood justice so  stay tuned for more updates
 

